WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.020
Getting a show from script to screen is
a fast and furious process. Some crews

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.980
make thirteen episodes of scripted TV
in the same amount of time it takes to

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.523
make a single blockbuster movie.

00:00:12.523 --> 00:00:14.220
So how do they do it?

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.060
This process applies to all types of scripted TV shows

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.120
– both dramas and comedies –

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.320
but we're gonna use an hourlong drama with a 13 episodes season as an example.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:30.240
Let's say you're the showrunner. This is what the next year of your life is going to look like.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.000
First thing's first: you and the writers break the story. Meaning you plot out the major

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.780
moments of the season. And then you assign  writers to break the story for each

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:44.230
episode. After multiple drafts a script around 45 pages is created. That's

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:48.620
about six hundred pages of material for
the whole season. At this point

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.280
episodes are assigned to directors and
directors of photography who begin

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.039
planning on their own and then there's
the art department. They're in charge of

00:00:56.039 --> 00:01:00.039
the whole look of the show. Let's say the
show takes place in New York City in

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:04.229
1980. The art department researchers the
period so that everything as big as

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:08.560
buildings and cars and as small
magazines and jewelry look realistic.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.100
Now let's get shooting.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.640
To shoot 45 pages of material in about 8 days you need a serious

00:01:14.649 --> 00:01:18.799
schedule. A traditional script isn't all
that helpful to a crew. So first

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:23.459
each scene is assigned a location, time of day, and actors. After all these factors

00:01:23.459 --> 00:01:29.259
are prioritized the result is a document
called a white schedule. Once shooting begins

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:33.579
every episode requires a table read.
That's when the whole cast gets together

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:36.560
and reads the script from beginning to
end.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.649
At this point the director works with the
director of photography to correctly

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:45.740
block all the actors and every scene is
shot from multiple angles. A one-minute

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:50.640
scene in a show could easily take a few
hours to shoot. Now you might not think

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.890
your typical drama would not have a lot of
visual effects but something as little

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:58.649
as a TV playing in the background of a
scene requires post-production work. Ever

00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:01.820
heard the phrase "we'll fix it in post."
Here we go.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.649
A post production team is comprised of
editors, sound designers, visual effects

00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:09.319
artists, and all the assistants and
coordinators that go along with them.

00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:12.580
Editors make the first cut of the
episode and give it to the director. Then

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:16.610
the director's cut goes to the showrunners.
The showrunners give notes, create a

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.299
producer's cut, and that goes to the
studio which then becomes the network

00:02:21.299 --> 00:02:25.500
cut. During this entire process things
like that TV with the green screen get

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.739
pushed through visual effects. Once all
of the edits are final, a color corrector and

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:33.620
sound designer begin to work their magic.
The dialogue needs to be cleaned up,

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.630
sound effects put in, and the entire edit
needs to be mixed. OK, we're almost done.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:43.739
It's now several months after writing
began and the episode, fully mastered, gets

00:02:43.739 --> 00:02:47.859
serviced to broadcasters around the
country. But the rest of the show isn't

00:02:47.859 --> 00:02:50.570
finished. Every episode is finalized

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:55.200
just a few weeks, and sometimes just a
few hours, before it premieres.

